  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Gliophorus perplexus is a small waxcap mushroom characterized by its delicate appearance and waxy gill texture. This species belongs to the diverse Hygrocybaceae family, known for their brightly colored fruiting bodies and preference for grassland habitats.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Waxy Gills",
      "description": "Possesses the characteristic waxy, non-blotting gills typical of waxcaps, which feel smooth and somewhat greasy to the touch",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Stature",
      "description": "Typically small mushrooms with delicate caps, usually under 2-3 cm in diameter with a slender stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Moist Habitats",
      "description": "Often displays moisture-dependent color changes and grows in wet to damp grassland conditions",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Gliophorus perplexus thrives in moist, unimproved grasslands and meadows throughout New England, preferring areas with rich moss and minimal chemical treatment.",
